'''way-door†''' (wā′dōr), ''n.'' A street-door.
{{smaller block|<poem>He must needs his posts with blood embrue,
And on his ''way-door'' fix the horned head.</poem>
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Satires, III. iv. 7.}}}}

'''wayfare''' (wā′fãr), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''weyfaren'', orig. in ppr. ''weyfarand'', < AS. ''wegfarende'' (= Icel. ''vegfarandi'' = Sw. ''vägfarande'' = Dan. ''vejfarende''), < ''weg'', way, + ''farende'', ppr. of ''faran'', go: see ''way''<sup>1</sup> and ''fare''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''wayfare'', ''n.''] To journey; travel, especially on foot: now only in the present participle or the verbal noun.
{{smaller block|A certain Laconian, as he ''way-fared'', came unto a place where there dwelt an old friend of his.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 390.}}
Farewell, honest Antony!—Pleasant be your ''wayfaring'', prosperous your return!
{{right|''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 416.}}}}

'''wayfarer''' (wā′fãr″ėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''weyfarere'', a wayfarer; < ''way''<sup>1</sup> + ''farer''.] One who wayfares, journeys, or travels; a traveler, especially one who travels on foot; a passenger. ''R. Carew.''
{{smaller block|The peasant is recommended [1362] to give to the needy ''wayfarer'' in preference to the beggar.
{{right|''Ribton-Turner'', Vagrants and Vagrancy, p. 54.}}
<poem>The ''wayfarer'', at noon reposing,
Shall bless its shadow on the grass.</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', On Planting a Tree at Inverara.}}}}

