'''wayless''' (wā′les), ''a.'' [< ''way''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less.''] Having no way or path; pathless; trackless.
{{smaller block|As though the peopled towns had ''way-less'' deserts been.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, ii. 164.}}}}

'''way-maker''' (wā′mā″kėr), ''n.'' One who makes a way; a pioneer; a pathfinder.
{{smaller block|Those famous ''way-makers'' to the . . . restitution of the evangelical truth. ''Bp. Hall'', Cases of Conscience, iii. 10.}}

'''way-mark''' (wā′märk), ''n.'' A finger-post, guide-post, milestone, or the like.
{{smaller block|She was so liable to fits of absence that she was likely enough to let her ''way-marks'' pass unnoticed.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, vi. 13.}}}}

'''wayment†''', '''waymenting†'''. See ''waiment'', ''waimenting''.

'''wayne<sup>1</sup>†''', ''n.'' An obsolete spelling of ''wain''<sup>1</sup>. ''Spenser'', F. Q., I. v. 41.

'''way-passenger''' (wā′pas″en-jėr), ''n.'' A passenger taken up or set down by the way—that is, at a way-station or at some place intermediate between the principal stopping-places or stations.

'''way-post''' (wā′pōst), ''n.'' A finger-post; a guide-post.
{{smaller block|You have more roads than a ''way-post''.
{{right|''Colman'', The Spleen, i. (''Davies.'')}}
<poem>An old ''way-post'' show’d
Where the Lavington road
Branch’d off to the left from the one to Devizes.</poem>
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, II. 172.}}}}
